Create an SVG diagram for a technical talk: "What customers buy vs. where it
actually runs". It is step 1 of a three-step build-up; steps 2 and 3 add to
this figure without moving anything, so all coordinates chosen here are final.

Step 1 shows only what each customer orders, not where it runs:
- 16:9 canvas (1200×675), white background, content fills it with only a
  slight margin.
- Top row: 3 customer pills. A buys a VM; B and C buy containers.
- Below each pill, what that order consists of:
  - A: an empty VM (green box) - A rents a machine and brings their own stack.
  - B: 2 generic containers.
  - C: 3 containers running one service each: nginx, postgres, JRE with a
    spring-boot service.
- Straight arrows from each customer to what they ordered. A's arrow stops at
  the VM boundary, B's and C's reach down to their containers, because they
  never ordered a VM. Labels must not overlap arrows. One colour per customer.
- One-line footer: "What each customer orders."
- Style: clean, light, CSS classes in <style>, green for VMs, purple for the
  virtualization layer.
- Deck style (must match the talk's CSS tokens exactly):
  - Fonts: text is 'IBM Plex Sans', sans-serif; monospace/code is
    'Source Code Pro', monospace. No Inter, no JetBrains Mono.
  - Diagram title: fill #8a0404 (deck heading red), font-weight 600,
    letter-spacing -0.035em.
  - Body text: fill #242126 (ink). Secondary/caption text: fill #706b6d
    (muted). Neutral hairlines/box borders: #e6e1df.
  - Background: #ffffff.
  - Accent colours stay semantic (see above) and are only used for the
    diagram's own boxes, never for the title or body text.
